WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.170
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:08.940
If we study the laws of fluid motion from
physics, and write a computer program that

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:14.519
contains these laws, we can create wondrous
fluid simulations like the ones you see here.

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:19.420
The amount of detail we can simulate with
these programs is increasing every year, not

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:24.779
only due to the fact that hardware improves
over time, but also, the pace of progress

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:27.679
in computer graphics research is truly remarkable.

00:00:27.679 --> 00:00:30.929
So, it s there nothing else to do?

00:00:30.929 --> 00:00:33.190
Are we done with fluid simulation research?

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.360
Oh, no.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.120
No, no, no.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.830
For instance, fluid-solid interaction still
remains a challenging phenomenon to simulate.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.780
This means that the sand is allowed to have
an effect on the fluid, but at the same time,

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.430
as the fluid sloshes around, it also moves
the sand particles within.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.440
This is what we refer to as two-way coupling.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.540
Note that this previous work that you see
here was built on the Material Point Method,

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:05.590
a hybrid simulation technique that uses both
particles and grids, whereas this new paper

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:11.050
introduces proper fluid-solid coupling to
the simpler, grid-based simulators.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:16.030
Not only that, but this new work also shows
us that there are different kinds of two-way

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.260
coupling.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:22.120
If we look at this footage with the honey
and the dipper, it looks great, however, this

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.440
still doesn’t seem right to me.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:29.890
We are doing science here, so fortunately,
we don’t need to guess what seems or doesn’t

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.890
seem right.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:36.170
This is my favorite part, because this is
when we let reality be our judge and compare

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.200
to what exactly happens in the real world.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.350
So let’s do that!

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.400
Whoa!

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:50.400
There is quite a bit of a difference, because
in reality, the honey is able to support the

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.460
dipper.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:57.280
One-way coupling, of course, cannot simulate
this kind of back and forth interaction, and

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.190
neither can weak two-way coupling pull this
off.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:06.560
And now, let’s see, YES!

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.180
There we go, the new strong two-way coupling
method finally gets this right.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:17.090
And not only that, but what I really love
about this is that it also gets small nuances

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:18.090
right.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.349
I will try to speed up the footage a little,
so you can see that the honey doesn’t only

00:02:22.349 --> 00:02:29.129
support the dipper, but the dipper still has
some subtle movements both in reality and

00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:30.840
in the simulation.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:31.849
A+.

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:32.849
Love it.

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:34.659
So, what is the problem?

00:02:34.659 --> 00:02:37.230
Why is this so difficult to simulate?

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:42.379
One of the key problems here is being able
to have a simulation that has a fine resolution

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:46.700
in the areas where a fluid and a solid intersect
each other.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.249
If we create a super detailed simulation,
it will take from hours to days to compute,

00:02:51.249 --> 00:02:56.560
but on the other hand, if we have a too coarse
one, it will compute the required deformations

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.900
in so few of these grid points that we’ll
get a really inaccurate simulation, and not

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.400
only that, but we will even miss some of the
interactions completely.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.529
This paper proposes a neat new volume estimation
technique that focuses these computations

00:03:12.529 --> 00:03:18.189
to where the action happens, and only there,
which means that we can get these really incredible

00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:23.299
results, even if we only run a relatively
coarse simulation.

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:27.829
I could watch these gooey, viscous simulations
all day long.

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:32.449
If you have a closer look at the paper in
the description, you will find some hard data

00:03:32.449 --> 00:04:21.329
that shows that this technique executes quicker
than other methods that are able to provide

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:31.210
comparable results.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:38.270
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

